system "ALA-LC:jpn-Hrkt:Latn:2022" {

metadata {
  authority_id alalc
  id 2022
  language iso-639-2:jpn
  source_script Hrkt
  destination_script Latn
  name "Japanese Romanization Table (2022)"
  url https://www.loc.gov/catdir/cpso/romanization/japanese.pdf
  creation_date 2022
  adoption_date
  description {    ALA-LC Japanese Romanization Table, 2022 version
    (McCune-Reischauer). Encodes the Romanized/Kana Equivalent Charts
    (chart A, Standard; chart B, Non-traditional Japanese for foreign
    words) kana-by-kana, per section 2.1 ("words and parts of words
    written only in hiragana or katakana should be romanized as they
    appear") and section 2.4.2.1 ("Romanize kana-by-kana"), together
    with the deterministic orthographic rules of the same document: the
    lengthening-bar macron rule (2.5.1), the sokuon doubling rule
    (chart A note ii), the n-apostrophe rule (5.2), the kana repetition
    markers (4.4.1) and the punctuation and typographical equivalents
    (4.2-4.3). Transcribed from the loc.gov document's complete text
    layer. }

  notes {
    note "Kanji readings are the non-deterministic remainder of this
    system (kanji entries were marked M for multiple readings in
    earlier editions of this table): sections 2.2-2.4 direct the
    cataloger to preferred readings from standard dictionaries,
    well-established readings of proper names, and loan-word
    conventions. This map covers the deterministic kana tables only."
    note "Long vowels written without a lengthening bar are a
    documented remainder: they merge into a macron only within a
    morpheme (2.5.5), and the document itself attests identical kana
    strings with different outputs (こうし as Kōshi in 孔子, merged, and
    as koushi in 子牛, unmerged). This map therefore romanizes
    kana-by-kana (こう -> ou), which is the document's stated primary
    mode for kana (2.4.2.1; e.g. スタア sutaa, ジイサン jiisan, びいどろ
    biidoro, ジウム jiumu). The lengthening bar ー is deterministic
    (2.5.1: vowels written with a lengthening bar are romanized with a
    macron) and is encoded; long i is doubled ii except after the bar
    (2.5.4: ジイサン jiisan, ビードロ bīdoro)."
    note "Particle readings are a documented remainder: は and へ used
    as grammatical particles romanize wa and e (chart A note i; section
    2.3.1). Reading the particle requires parsing, so this map gives
    the chart values ha and he."
    note "Capitalization (section 3) and word division (section 6) are
    cataloging-level rules operating above the character mapping and
    are not encoded; this map outputs lowercase romanization with no
    word division."
    note "Chart A 'Other Romanization Styles' footnote markers, shown
    for reference only and not encoded: 1 shi (si in Kunrei, Nippon);
    2 chi, tsu (ti, tu in Kunrei, Nippon); 3 fu (hu in Kunrei, Nippon);
    4 を/ヲ 'Always o regardless of usage'; 5 ん/ン 'Always n (Do not
    change to m as in Hepburn)'; 6 ji (zi in Kunrei, Nippon); 7 ji, zu
    (zi, zu in Kunrei; di, du in Nippon); 8 sha, shu, sho (sya, syu,
    syo in Kunrei, Nippon); 9 cha, chu, cho (tya, tyu, tyo in Kunrei,
    Nippon); 10 ja, ju, jo (zya, zyu, zyo in Kunrei, Nippon); 11 ja,
    ju, jo (zya, zyu, zyo in Kunrei; dya, dyu, dyo in Nippon). The
    ALA-LC forms are the ones encoded."
    note "Chart A also maps the C + small-い cells to a macron i as
    printed (きぃ/キィ ki with macron; likewise ni, hi, mi, ri, gi, bi,
    pi rows), and the ぁ/ぉ small-kana cells of the ki, hi, bi, pi rows
    (きぁ kya, ひぉ hyo, びぉ byo, ぴぉ pyo). Chart B note iii directs
    that large character-small character sequences not found in the
    tables be romanized as if the added small characters were regular
    characters (e.g. アィ ai, スェ sue, ヴィァ via); this map realizes
    the rule by folding the standalone small vowels ぁぃぅぇぉ ァィゥェォ
    to their regular equivalents."
    note "Recorded but not encoded: the middle dot (4.1: comma plus
    space or only a space, depending on clarity), the kanji iteration
    mark 々 of the 4.4.1 examples (chōchō, hibi, nennen - kanji-level
    repetition), and the geta placeholder (4.4.2, cataloger supplies
    the reading in brackets). Section 2.3.2 (historical kana usage) is
    covered by the kana-by-kana mode: direct kana-by-kana reading gives
    the suggested variant forms (てふてふ tefutefu)."
    note "The document contradicts itself on スェ: chart B prints the
    row すぇ/スェ -> swe, while chart B note iii's worked example
    prints スェ | sue. The chart row is operative here (swe); the note
    iii examples アィ -> ai and ヴィァ -> via are consistent and encoded
    through the small-kana fold."
  }
}

